<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xue-Cheng Tai
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>NORCE Norwegian Research Centre, Nygårdstangen, NO-5838 Bergen, Norway Email: xtai@norceresearch.no, xuechengtai@gmail.com.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hao Liu
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Mathematics, Hong Kong Baptist University, Kowloon Tong, Hong Kong. Email: haoliu@hkbu.edu.hk.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lingfeng Li
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Hong Kong Centre for Cerebro-Cardiovascular Health Engineering, Shatin, Hong Kong. Email: lfli@hkcoche.org.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Raymond H. Chan
</span><span id="id4" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>School of Data Science and Department of Operations and Risk Management, Lingnan University, Tuen Mun, Hong Kong. Email: raymond.chan@ln.edu.hk.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The Transformer architecture has revolutionized the field of sequence modeling and underpins the recent breakthroughs in large language models (LLMs). However, a comprehensive mathematical theory that explains its structure and operations remains elusive. In this work, we propose a novel continuous framework that rigorously interprets the Transformer as a discretization of a structured integro-differential equation. Within this formulation, the self-attention mechanism emerges naturally as a non-local integral operator, and layer normalization is characterized as a projection to a time-dependent constraint. This operator-theoretic and variational perspective offers a unified and interpretable foundation for understanding the architecture’s core components, including attention, feedforward layers, and normalization. Our approach extends beyond previous theoretical analyses by embedding the entire Transformer operation in continuous domains for both token indices and feature dimensions. This leads to a principled and flexible framework that not only deepens theoretical insight but also offers new directions for architecture design, analysis, and control-based interpretations. This new interpretation provides a step toward bridging the gap between deep learning architectures and continuous mathematical modeling, and contributes a foundational perspective to the ongoing development of interpretable and theoretically grounded neural network models.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key words:</span> transformer, attention, operator splitting, integro-differential equation</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deep neural networks (DNNs) have revolutionized numerous fields, including natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, scientific computing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and medical diagnostics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Among these architectures, Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> have recently emerged as particularly powerful tools, underpinning remarkable successes in large language models (LLMs) such as GPT-3 and GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. Besides language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, the Transformer and its variants have also been applied in applications, including image processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, graph processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, operator learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, a series of works have been conducted to study the theoretical foundation or interpretability of Transformers. Approximation and generalization error of Transformers were studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. It was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> that Transformers are adaptive to data’s low-dimensional structures. For interpretability, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, it was shown that Transformers are cubic or high-order splines. It was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> that the vision Transformer can learn spatial structures of the dataset. The Transformer was interpreted as an ODE solver for multi-particle dynamical systems in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. Meng et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> showed that Transformer is a step for solving a nonlocal variational model. A general introduction to Transformers with mathematical descriptions can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to Transformers, more studies can be found for deep neural networks (DNNs). A widely accepted interpretation views DNN as nonlinear high-dimensional function approximators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Under this view, the layers of a DNN perform successive nonlinear transformations that approximate complex mappings from input to output data. This approximation framework has been instrumental in explaining the expressive power of DNNs.
Another promising direction for explaining and designing neural network architectures involves interpreting them as discretizations of continuous-time dynamical systems governed by differential or integral equations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>.
E et al. show that DNNs can be viewed as continuous dynamical systems in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and special discretization of continuous problems in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Chen et al. pointed out connections between residual networks and ordinary differential equations, and proposed Neural ODE. Benning et al. investigated the relations between DNNs and optimal control problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.
In this class of approach, neural network training is seen as an optimal control problem constrained by a continuous evolution equation.
Related to the above-mentioned approach, we especially want to mention some recent studies that have proposed a general framework for designing neural network architectures inspired by continuous-time dynamical systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. These methods leverage operator-splitting techniques to construct neural networks, which are so-called continuous neural networks, as discretizations of continuous differential equations. The continuous learning problem is an optimal control problem with this "neural network" as a constraint. In this framework, the evolution of hidden states in a network is governed by differential equations (continuous neural network). The goal of this continuous learning problem is to find optimal controls—represented by learnable parameters—within a discretized dynamical process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The use of operator-splitting allows these complex dynamics to be decomposed into simpler substeps, each of which corresponds to a specific type of operation (e.g., convolution, nonlinearity, normalization). These substeps are then unrolled into individual layers of a neural network. This interpretation not only clarifies the structure and purpose of different architectural components but also facilitates the incorporation of prior knowledge about the underlying data dynamics or physics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Building on this theory, we have recently provided a rigorous mathematical explanation of the well-known UNet architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. We demonstrated that UNet can be viewed as a specific discretization of the following simple differential equation, where each encoder and decoder stage corresponds to a particular substep in the operator-splitting scheme:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\frac{\partial u(\mathbf{x},t)}{\partial t}=W(\mathbf{x},t)\ast u(\mathbf{x},t)+d(t)-\ln\frac{u(\mathbf{x},t)}{1-u(\mathbf{x},t)}+\partial I_{\Sigma}(u),\ (\mathbf{x},t)\in\Omega\times(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},0)=f(\mathbf{x}),\ \mathbf{x}\in\Omega,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>ln</mi><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo>∂</mo><mi>I</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\frac{\partial u(\mathbf{x},t)}{\partial t}=W(\mathbf{x},t)\ast u(\mathbf{x},t)+d(t)-\ln\frac{u(\mathbf{x},t)}{1-u(\mathbf{x},t)}+\partial I_{\Sigma}(u),\ (\mathbf{x},t)\in\Omega\times(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},0)=f(\mathbf{x}),\ \mathbf{x}\in\Omega,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math> denotes convolution, <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\{u:u(\mathbf{x})\geq 0\mbox{ for }\mathbf{x}\in\Omega\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\{u:u(\mathbf{x})\geq 0\mbox{ for }\mathbf{x}\in\Omega\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a function defined in a domain <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the final time chosen by the user for time variable <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The learnable variables <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\{W(\mathbf{x},t),d(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\{W(\mathbf{x},t),d(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are regarded as control variables which will be learned in the training process. The mapping <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{\theta}(f)\mapsto u(\mathbf{x},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{\theta}(f)\mapsto u(\mathbf{x},T)</annotation></semantics></math> turns out to be the UNet in the continuous setting.
This perspective not only elucidates the structure of the UNet architecture but also provides a foundation for systematic modifications and enhancements. A range of alternative interpretations and mathematically inspired designs of deep neural networks can be found in the literature, including <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. For example, neural networks have been interpreted as optimal control problems in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. Gregor and LeCun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> were among the first to connect deep learning with sparse coding through learned iterative shrinkage algorithms. Ruthotto and Haber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> proposed neural networks derived from partial differential equations (PDEs), while He and Xu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> introduced MgNet, which employs multigrid principles for feature extraction. Hagemann et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> formulated stochastic normalizing flows as Markov chains to tackle inverse problems. Long et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> built PDENet based on finite difference schemes to recover PDE models from data. Yang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> constructed ADMM-CSNet by unrolling the ADMM optimization algorithm into a network architecture. Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> developed a diffusion-residual network inspired by convection-diffusion ODEs for classification tasks. Most recently, Martin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> introduced a plug-and-play architecture using flow matching for image restoration. Liu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> proposed a novel network regularizer inspired by the reverse process of PDE-based evolution models. Networks regularized by prior information are proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. These pioneering efforts have provided essential insights and have directly inspired our recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> and the present study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we will develop a mathematical theoretical framework to explain the Transformer architecture of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, which has achieved remarkable success in large language models (LLMs) and other sequence modeling tasks. We show that the Transformer can be interpreted as a discretization of the following continuous integro-differential equation:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}}_{\mbox{I: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t))}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer normalization}}\\
\hskip 85.35826pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}}\mbox{ for }t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}),\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>I: attention</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>II: layer normalization</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>III: fully connected network</mtext></msub><mtext> for </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}}_{\mbox{I: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t))}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer normalization}}\\
\hskip 85.35826pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}}\mbox{ for }t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}),\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\in\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\in\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the token index, <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> the entry of token vectors, <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are continuous domains, and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed terminal time.
This formulation provides a rigorous mathematical interpretation of the Transformer and its key components, such as self-attention and feedforward layers. The definitions of attention, layer normalization, and the fully connected layer, along with detailed explanations of the domains <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the control variables, are given in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Equation (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) highlights the non-local nature of attention and reveals the Transformer as a structured operator derived from a discretized variational principle.
We note that several previous works have also established connections between Transformer-based models and multi-particle dynamical systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. These studies interpret the original or modified Transformer models as discretizations of interacting particle systems in a time-continuous setting, treating interactions across spatial variables as particle interactions. In contrast, our formulation (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is fundamentally different: we show that the Transformer arises as a discretization of a continuous integro-differential equation, offering a more unified operator-theoretic and variational perspective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This perspective offers several advantages. First, it provides a unifying mathematical framework that connects diverse architectures, such as CNNs, UNets, and Transformers, under the common lens of differential and integral equations. This unification improves our understanding of the design principles underlying modern deep networks and provides insights for cross-architectural studies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Second, by casting neural network architectures as time-stepping schemes of dynamical systems, our framework allows the systematic exploration of new architectures using well-established tools from numerical analysis. For instance, stability, convergence, and approximation properties of the underlying continuous models can inform the selection of network structures and hyperparameters, leading to more robust and interpretable models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Third, this approach creates a principled pathway for embedding domain-specific knowledge, such as physical laws, geometric structures, or conservation principles, directly into the design of neural architectures, leading to architectures tailored for specific scientific or engineering tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our formulation not only advances the theoretical understanding of deep neural networks but also provides actionable tools for the principled design of next-generation models. It bridges the gap between continuous mathematical modeling and discrete network implementation, establishing a foundation for informed, explainable, and application-aware neural network development.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is organized as follows: We introduce the integral equation for Transformer with single-head attention in continuous setting in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. An operator-splitting based algorithm is proposed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to solve the integral equation. We show in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Mathematical Explanations of Transformers ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that the discretized algorithm exactly recovers the Transformer encoder in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, and also discuss how to adapt the proposed algorithm to recover the architecture of ViT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we extend the integral equation and proposed algorithm to Transformer with multi-head attention. Extension to convolutional Transformer and its relation to CvT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> are discussed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Convolution Transformers for Video and Image Problems ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. This paper is concluded in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Conclusion ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In the rest of this paper, we use bold lowercase letters to denote vectors, normal letters to denote scalars and functions, and calligraphic letters to denote operators.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>The Continuous Model</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we explain the Transformer in the seminal work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> in the continuous setting and show later that proper discretizations of the proposed model will recover the Transformer exactly.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>The continuous setting for Transformers</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose a control problem in a continuous setting. The continuous Transformer is a time-dependent integral equation. Later, we will show that after discretizations by operator splitting for the time variable <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and spatial discretizations for the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> variables, the resulting splitting method recovers the Transformer architecture.
The Transformer proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> consists of three components: attention, layer normalization, and feedforward networks. In the derivation of our control problem, we show that each component has a corresponding operation in the integral equation. We first focus on single-head attention. Our framework can be easily extended to the multi-head case, which will be detailed in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To make the explanation clearer and simpler, our notations and setup will follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, but in a continuous counterpart. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math> be a function of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and time <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. In applications of LLMs, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\Omega_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\Omega_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the index of tokens, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to entries of token vectors. Please refer to Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Explanation of the variables for Transformers ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for explanations of the physical meanings of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> for applications of Transformers to LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As stated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, attention is all you need for the Transformer. In fact, we show below that the attention layer has a very simple mathematical explanation in the continuous setting. It consists of integral transformations on the input functions to extract features with three different kernels. We use features extracted from the first two kernels to generate the attention score through a softmax operator, and then multiply the attention score by the feature extracted from the third kernel to compute the output. All three kernels in the integral transformation will be parameterized as learnable variables, see (<a href="#S2.E10" title="In 2.5 The continuous control problem for learning ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Integral Transformations for Attention and Feature Extraction</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let the functions <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{K}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{K}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{V}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{V}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)</annotation></semantics></math> be three kernels defined on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{y}\times[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{y}\times[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>, and they will be learned from data as shown in LLM applications.
See Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Explanation of the variables for Transformers ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for explanations of the physical meanings of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a given function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define integral transformations:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{Q}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{Q}(\xi,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{Q}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{Q}(\xi,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{K}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{K}(\xi,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{K}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{K}(\xi,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{V}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{V}(\xi,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{V}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{V}(\xi,\mathbf{y},t)u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here and later, we use <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\Omega_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\Omega_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> inner product on a given domain <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its induced norm will be denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\Omega_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\Omega_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Functions <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> will be used to generate attention scores, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is used to extract features from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. When all these functions are discretized over a pixel-type grid over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the discrete counterparts of these functions are represented by matrices and the discrete counterparts of the integral transformations are just standard matrix multiplications, see Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, especially formula (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.4.1 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{1/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). We compute the attention score by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)=\text{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),\ K(\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},\cdot,t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)=\text{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),\ K(\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},\cdot,t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{x}\times[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{x}\times[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Softmax}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Softmax}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the softmax function along the second dimension:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Softmax}_{2}(a(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t))=\frac{\exp(a(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t))}{\int_{\Omega_{x}}\exp(a(\mathbf{x},\eta,t))d\eta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Softmax}_{2}(a(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t))=\frac{\exp(a(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t))}{\int_{\Omega_{x}}\exp(a(\mathbf{x},\eta,t))d\eta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The output of the attention layer is then obtained by taking the inner product of the attention score <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and the feature <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}.</annotation></semantics></math>
We emphasize that the first two integral transformations in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2 Integral Transformations for Attention and Feature Extraction ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.2 Integral Transformations for Attention and Feature Extraction ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) are used to generate the attention score <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)</annotation></semantics></math> and the last integral transformation (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2 Integral Transformations for Attention and Feature Extraction ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) will be used for feature extractions. All three integral kernels will be learned during training, see explanations later in this section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Mathematical formulations for the Layer Normalization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The layer normalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> in the Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> and also many other neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> can be mathematically described as a projection of a function to a set with given mean value <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define sets</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})=\bigg\{u:\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi=\sigma_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})=\bigg\{u:\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)d\xi=\sigma_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip 142.26378pt\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi=\sigma_{2}^{2}\bigg\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip 142.26378pt\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi,t)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi=\sigma_{2}^{2}\bigg\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{2}=\{u:u\geq 0\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{2}=\{u:u\geq 0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and their corresponding indicator functions</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}(u)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\mbox{ if }u\in S_{1},\\
+\infty&amp;\mbox{ otherwise,}\end{cases}\quad I_{S_{2}}(u)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\mbox{ if }u\in S_{2},\\
+\infty&amp;\mbox{ otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise,</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}(u)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\mbox{ if }u\in S_{1},\\
+\infty&amp;\mbox{ otherwise,}\end{cases}\quad I_{S_{2}}(u)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\mbox{ if }u\in S_{2},\\
+\infty&amp;\mbox{ otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Later, we shall show that layer normalization is just a projection of a function into the set <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, see Section <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS2" title="3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.2</span></a> and the ReLU activation function is just a projection of a function to the set <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Section <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS3" title="3.3.3 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>The continuous Transformer</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the control variables <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{K}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{K}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{V}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{V}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, let us also introduce control variables <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W_{j}(\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},\mathbf{y},t)\}_{j=1}^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W_{j}(\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},\mathbf{y},t)\}_{j=1}^{J}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{y}\times[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{y}\times[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\mathbf{x},t),b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\mathbf{x},t),b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}\times[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}\times[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We call the following continuous integral equation a <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Continuous Transformer</span>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),\ V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}}_{\mbox{I: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t))}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer normalization}}\\
\hskip 28.45274pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}},\quad t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}).\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>I: attention</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>II: layer normalization</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>III: fully connected network</mtext></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),\ V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}}_{\mbox{I: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t))}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer normalization}}\\
\hskip 28.45274pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}},\quad t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We shall show in the rest of this paper that the Transformer proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> is just a discretization of this continuous Transformer.
In (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is some initial state, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed time chosen by the user. Following conventions in the literature, we denote all the control variables as</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\left\{W^{Q}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),W^{K}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),W^{V}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),\{W_{j}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\}_{j=1}^{J},\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t)\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\left\{W^{Q}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),W^{K}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),W^{V}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),\{W_{j}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t),b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\}_{j=1}^{J},\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t)\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The Continuous Transformer is the mapping:</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{\theta}:f\mapsto u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{\theta}:f\mapsto u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we will discretize (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) by the operator-splitting method for the time variable and use a uniform grid for the spatial variables <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. After discretization, the continuous Transformer becomes the Transformer proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. In relation to this equivalence, the operations in the right-hand side of (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) can be classified into three sets:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Operations in Set I correspond to attentions in Transformers. It computes the attention score <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)</annotation></semantics></math> and applies it to the extracted features in <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Operations in Set II correspond to layer normalization. For each token location <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, it requires function <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to have mean <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> along the <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> direction. In other words, for each token, the corresponding series is expected to have mean <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Operations in Set III correspond to feedforward networks with <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> layers. The first component corresponds to linear layers, each of which contains one linear transformation <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and a bias <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The second component corresponds to activation functions. This term projects <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be realized by ReLU functions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>The continuous control problem for learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our objective is to optimize <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> so that given an input <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, the control problem will drive <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},T)</annotation></semantics></math> to a desired state <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, given a dataset <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(u_{i},v_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(u_{i},v_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the target state, let <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> be a loss function measuring the discrepancy between its arguments.
Let <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta,\mathcal{N}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta,\mathcal{N}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> be as defined in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E9" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), the training process with the loss function <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact a process to optimize <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> for the following minimization problem:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\theta}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(\mathcal{N}_{\theta}(u_{i}),v_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\theta}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(\mathcal{N}_{\theta}(u_{i}),v_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is essentially a PDE-constrained optimization problem of the following form:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\theta}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(w_{i},v_{i}),\mbox{ under constraint }w_{i}=\mathcal{N}_{\theta}(u_{i})\mbox{ solves the control problem (\ref{eq.control})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> under constraint </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> solves the control problem (</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\theta}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(w_{i},v_{i}),\mbox{ under constraint }w_{i}=\mathcal{N}_{\theta}(u_{i})\mbox{ solves the control problem (\ref{eq.control})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Discretizations of the Continuous Problem</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the idea of PottsMGNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, in this section, we use some proper operator splitting methods to discretize the time variable for (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
For a review about operator splitting methods, please refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, we discretize the temporal domain of (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) by sequential splitting, c.f. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Sec. 2.2)</cite>. The number of sequential splittings corresponds to the number of layers in Transformers. Spatial discretization for <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> will be discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. The discretization of <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> determines the number of tokens in the input, and the discretization of <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> determines the size of the embedding vector for each token.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>The overall time discretization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t^{n}\}_{n=0}^{N_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t^{n}\}_{n=0}^{N_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of time grids with time step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=T/N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=T/N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the numerical solution of (<a href="#S2.E10" title="In 2.5 The continuous control problem for learning ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) at time <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will design a numerical scheme to approximate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for each <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we design a discrete propagator <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> propagating <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, problem (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is numerically approximated by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u(t^{N_{t}})\approx u^{N_{t}}=\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}}\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}-1}\circ\cdots\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{1}(f)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(t^{N_{t}})\approx u^{N_{t}}=\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}}\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}-1}\circ\cdots\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{1}(f),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">see Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.4.4 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(2+𝑗)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b) for an illustration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, time-discretized control variables <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> will be used. We denote the control variables used in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To show the dependence of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on the control variable <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will also use <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}_{\theta^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}_{\theta^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the propagator <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
From the following subsections, c.f. §<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we will see that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{n}=\{W^{Q,n-1},W^{K,n-1},W^{V,n-1},\{W_{j}^{n-1},b_{j}^{n-1}\}_{j=1}^{J},\sigma_{1}^{n-1},\sigma_{2}^{n-1}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{n}=\{W^{Q,n-1},W^{K,n-1},W^{V,n-1},\{W_{j}^{n-1},b_{j}^{n-1}\}_{j=1}^{J},\sigma_{1}^{n-1},\sigma_{2}^{n-1}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Denote all of the discretized variables by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\theta}=\{\theta^{n}\}_{n=1}^{N_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\theta}=\{\theta^{n}\}_{n=1}^{N_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The whole evolution procedure from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{N_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{N_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}_{\bar{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}_{\bar{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i,e,
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}_{\bar{\theta}}=\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}}_{\theta^{N_{t}}}\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}-1}_{\theta^{N_{t}-1}}\circ\cdots\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{1}_{\theta^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}_{\bar{\theta}}=\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}}_{\theta^{N_{t}}}\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{N_{t}-1}_{\theta^{N_{t}-1}}\circ\cdots\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{1}_{\theta^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(u_{i},v_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(u_{i},v_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the discrete training problem is to solve the following constrained optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\bar{\theta}}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(\bar{\mathcal{N}}_{\bar{\theta}}(u_{i}),v_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\bar{\theta}}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(\bar{\mathcal{N}}_{\bar{\theta}}(u_{i}),v_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following sections, we illustrate our numerical scheme for propagator <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{1}_{\theta^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{1}_{\theta^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Propagator <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}_{\theta^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{n}_{\theta^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2,...,N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2,...,N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the same by replacing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our numerical scheme is designed based on operator splitting methods.
For simplicity, we take <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1</annotation></semantics></math>. It is often used in data analysis and image processing literature and is equivalent to changing the network parameters properly.
In the following, with a slight abuse of notation, we denote the time-discretized version of any time-dependent function <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(\cdot,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(\cdot,t)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{n}(\cdot):=a(\cdot,t^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{n}(\cdot):=a(\cdot,t^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th time step.
Using the Lie scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Sec. 2.2)</cite> as explained in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C An Introduction to Operator Splitting Schemes ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, given <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{0}=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{0}=f</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=4+J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=4+J</annotation></semantics></math> substeps:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substep 1</span>:
Solve <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1/M}</annotation></semantics></math> from</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{1/M}-u^{0}={\Big\langle}\gamma^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot;u^{0}),\ V^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y};u^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{1/M}-u^{0}={\Big\langle}\gamma^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot;u^{0}),\ V^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y};u^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substep 2</span>: Compute <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2/M}</annotation></semantics></math> from</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{2/M}-u^{1/M}=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}^{0},\sigma_{2}^{0})}(u^{2/M})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{2/M}-u^{1/M}=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}^{0},\sigma_{2}^{0})}(u^{2/M}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substep 2+j </span>:
Compute <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(2+j)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(2+j)/M}</annotation></semantics></math> sequentially for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,...,J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,...,J</annotation></semantics></math> from</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{(2+j)/M}-u^{(1+j)/M}={\Big\langle}W_{j}^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y}),\ u^{(1+j)/M}(\mathbf{x},\cdot){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b^{0}_{j}(\mathbf{x})+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u^{(2+j)/M})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{(2+j)/M}-u^{(1+j)/M}={\Big\langle}W_{j}^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y}),\ u^{(1+j)/M}(\mathbf{x},\cdot){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b^{0}_{j}(\mathbf{x})+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u^{(2+j)/M}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substep 3+J</span>:
Compute <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(3+J)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(3+J)/M}</annotation></semantics></math> from</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{(3+J)/M}=\frac{1}{2}(u^{(2+J)/M}+u^{2/M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{(3+J)/M}=\frac{1}{2}(u^{(2+J)/M}+u^{2/M})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substep 4+J</span>:
Compute <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{1}-u^{(3+J)/M}=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}^{0},\sigma_{2}^{0})}(u^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{1}-u^{(3+J)/M}=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}^{0},\sigma_{2}^{0})}(u^{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), the left hand side <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(k+1)/M}-u^{k/M}=\frac{u^{(k+1)/M}-u^{k/M}}{\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(k+1)/M}-u^{k/M}=\frac{u^{(k+1)/M}-u^{k/M}}{\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1</annotation></semantics></math> is the finite difference approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Substep 1 to Substep <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4+J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4+J)</annotation></semantics></math> evolve <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by sequentially passing it through all operators in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), corresponding to all components in Transformer from input to output. Substep 1 corresponds to the attention layer and the skip connection. Substep 2 corresponds to layer normalization. Substep 3 to Substep <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4+J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4+J)</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> layers in the fully connected network. Substep <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3+J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3+J</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the skip connection after the fully connected network. The last substep corresponds to the final layer normalization. From here, we can already see that each substep in the Lie splitting scheme corresponds to one layer in the Transformer. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, it used <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=2</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=6</annotation></semantics></math>. The different layers in the Transformer is just a sequential computation of the substep solutions:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{0}\quad\text{(input token)}\mapsto u^{1/6}\text{(attention)}\mapsto u^{2/6}\text{(normalization layer)}\\
\mapsto u^{3/6}\mbox{ and }u^{4/6}\text{(fully connected layer with ReLU)}\\
\mapsto u^{5/6}\text{(skip connection layer)}\mapsto u^{1}\text{(normalization layer)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mspace style="width:2.000em;" width="2.000em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>(input token)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(attention)</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(normalization layer)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(fully connected layer with ReLU)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(skip connection layer)</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(normalization layer)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{0}\quad\text{(input token)}\mapsto u^{1/6}\text{(attention)}\mapsto u^{2/6}\text{(normalization layer)}\\
\mapsto u^{3/6}\mbox{ and }u^{4/6}\text{(fully connected layer with ReLU)}\\
\mapsto u^{5/6}\text{(skip connection layer)}\mapsto u^{1}\text{(normalization layer)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Solutions to Each Subproblem</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we supply the details for the solution to each subproblem in the splitting scheme (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Solution to Subproblem (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{1/M}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{1/M}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle u^{0}+\int_{\Omega_{x}}\mathrm{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot;u^{0}),\ K^{0}(\eta,\cdot;u^{0};){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right)V^{0}(\eta,\mathbf{y};u^{0})d\eta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>Softmax</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{0}+\int_{\Omega_{x}}\mathrm{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot;u^{0}),\ K^{0}(\eta,\cdot;u^{0};){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right)V^{0}(\eta,\mathbf{y};u^{0})d\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This step is a continuous version of the attention layer in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. It looks very simple and uses two integral transformations to get the attention score through a softmax operator. This score function has values between <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and it is multiplied with the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{0}(\eta,\mathbf{y};u^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{0}(\eta,\mathbf{y};u^{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are the extracted features from the input data through another integral transformation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Solution to Subproblem (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2/M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in problem (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), they are in the form of</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u-v=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u-v=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}(u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for the given function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math> and constants <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is easy to derive that the solution <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) solves the following problem:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u=\mathop{\mathrm{argmin}}_{\bar{u}\in S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}\frac{1}{2}\|\bar{u}-v\|_{\Omega_{y}}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u=\mathop{\mathrm{argmin}}_{\bar{u}\in S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}\frac{1}{2}\|\bar{u}-v\|_{\Omega_{y}}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This shows that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a projection of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to the set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The following theorem gives a closed-form solution formula for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The solution to problem (<a href="#S3.E21" title="In 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) is given as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\frac{v(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\alpha(\mathbf{x})}{\sqrt{\beta(\mathbf{x})}}\sigma_{2}+\sigma_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\frac{v(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\alpha(\mathbf{x})}{\sqrt{\beta(\mathbf{x})}}\sigma_{2}+\sigma_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">with</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha(\mathbf{x};v)=\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi,\quad\beta(\mathbf{x};v)=\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(v(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\alpha(\mathbf{x}))^{2}d\xi." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha(\mathbf{x};v)=\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi,\quad\beta(\mathbf{x};v)=\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(v(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\alpha(\mathbf{x}))^{2}d\xi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is proved in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. We remark that the expression (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) recovers the normalization layer in the continuous setting.
According to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we see that the solutions <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2/M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(4+J)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(4+J)/M}</annotation></semantics></math> are explicitly given as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{2/M}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\frac{u^{1/M}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\alpha(\mathbf{x};u^{1/M})}{\sqrt{\beta(\mathbf{x};u^{1/M})}}\sigma_{2}^{0}+\sigma_{1}^{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{2/M}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\frac{u^{1/M}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\alpha(\mathbf{x};u^{1/M})}{\sqrt{\beta(\mathbf{x};u^{1/M})}}\sigma_{2}^{0}+\sigma_{1}^{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{1}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\frac{u^{(3+J)/M}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\alpha(\mathbf{x};u^{(3+J)/M})}{\sqrt{\beta(\mathbf{x};u^{(3+J)/M})}}\sigma_{2}^{0}+\sigma_{1}^{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{1}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\frac{u^{(3+J)/M}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\alpha(\mathbf{x};u^{(3+J)/M})}{\sqrt{\beta(\mathbf{x};u^{(3+J)/M})}}\sigma_{2}^{0}+\sigma_{1}^{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.3 </span>Solution to Subproblem (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Problem (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) is a semi-implicit equation for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(2+j)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(2+j)/M}</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be solved exactly by the following sequential splitting</p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}=u^{(1+j)/M}+{\Big\langle}W_{j}^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y}),\ u^{(1+j)/M}(\mathbf{x},\cdot){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}^{0}(\mathbf{x}),\\
u^{(2+j)/M}-\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}=\partial I_{S_{2}}(u^{(2+j)/M}).\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}=u^{(1+j)/M}+{\Big\langle}W_{j}^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y}),\ u^{(1+j)/M}(\mathbf{x},\cdot){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}^{0}(\mathbf{x}),\\
u^{(2+j)/M}-\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}=\partial I_{S_{2}}(u^{(2+j)/M}).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The first equation in (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.3.3 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is linear in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to a linear layer in a feed-forward network in continuous settings. The second equation solves</p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{(2+j)/M}=\mathop{\mathrm{argmin}}_{v\in S_{1}}\int_{\Omega_{x}}\int_{\Omega_{y}}|v-\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}|^{2}d\mathbf{y}d\mathbf{x}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{(2+j)/M}=\mathop{\mathrm{argmin}}_{v\in S_{1}}\int_{\Omega_{x}}\int_{\Omega_{y}}|v-\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}|^{2}d\mathbf{y}d\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">It is easy to see that its solution can be computed point-wisely:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{(2+j)/M}=\max\{\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M},0\}=\mathrm{ReLU}(\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{(2+j)/M}=\max\{\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M},0\}=\mathrm{ReLU}(\bar{u}^{(2+j)/M}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which corresponds ReLU activation. As a result, Substep <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+j</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,...,J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,...,J</annotation></semantics></math> is a feedforward network layer activated by ReLU.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Spatial Discretizations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we discretize the solution discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> spatially. We will show that after spatial discretizations, the splitting scheme (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) exactly recovers the Transformer architecture in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}=[0,L_{x}],\ \Omega_{y}=[0,L_{y}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}=[0,L_{x}],\ \Omega_{y}=[0,L_{y}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We uniformly discretize <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x},\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x},\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> grids in the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates, respectively. For simplicity, we suppose <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{x}=N_{x},L_{y}=N_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{x}=N_{x},L_{y}=N_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to discretization steps <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta x=L_{x}/N_{x}=1,\ \Delta y=L_{y}/N_{y}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta x=L_{x}/N_{x}=1,\ \Delta y=L_{y}/N_{y}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the grids by <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}=k,\mathbf{y}_{k}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}=k,\mathbf{y}_{k}=k</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{1}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{1}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote an <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector with elements 1. For a matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a vector <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=(\mathbf{W}+\bm{b})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=(\mathbf{W}+\bm{b})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed so that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{k,l}=W_{k,l}+b_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{k,l}=W_{k,l}+b_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This is an easy notation to add a vector to a matrix in the discrete setting.
With the spatial discretizations above, we define the discrete integrals (sum) as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}}fd\mathbf{x}=\sum_{k=1}^{N_{x}}f_{k},\quad\int_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}}\int_{\bar{\Omega}_{y}}f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})d\mathbf{x}d\mathbf{y}=\sum_{k=1}^{N_{x}}\sum_{l=1}^{N_{y}}f_{k,l}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}}fd\mathbf{x}=\sum_{k=1}^{N_{x}}f_{k},\quad\int_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}}\int_{\bar{\Omega}_{y}}f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})d\mathbf{x}d\mathbf{y}=\sum_{k=1}^{N_{x}}\sum_{l=1}^{N_{y}}f_{k,l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For any functions <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\mathbf{x}),w(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\mathbf{x}),w(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote their discrete counterpart by <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v},\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v},\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the discretized inner product between <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\Big\langle}\mathbf{v},\mathbf{w}{\Big\rangle}_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}}=\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{w}=\sum_{k=1}^{N_{x}}v_{k}w_{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\Big\langle}\mathbf{v},\mathbf{w}{\Big\rangle}_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}}=\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{w}=\sum_{k=1}^{N_{x}}v_{k}w_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The discretized integrals and inner products <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\bar{\Omega}_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\bar{\Omega}_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}\times\bar{\Omega}_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\bar{\Omega}_{x}\times\bar{\Omega}_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined similarly.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.1 </span>Computing the Discrete Solutions <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1/M}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In 3.3.1 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before we discretize the formula (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In 3.3.1 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), we first discretize some related quantities and operations.
To compute the attention scores, we discretize the integral transformations as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\quad\mathbf{K}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{K,0},\quad\mathbf{V}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{V,0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\quad\mathbf{K}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{K,0},\quad\mathbf{V}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{V,0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{V}^{Q,0},\mathbf{K}^{Q,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{V}^{Q,0},\mathbf{K}^{Q,0}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the expressions such as <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}</annotation></semantics></math> represent standard matrix multiplications that yield new matrices in <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We emphasize that the multiplication used here is the conventional matrix product.
For <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\{a_{k,l}\}_{k,l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\{a_{k,l}\}_{k,l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the discretized version of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Softmax}_{2}(\mathbf{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Softmax</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Softmax}_{2}(\mathbf{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is given as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\mathrm{Softmax}_{2,{\rm dis}}(\mathbf{A})\right)_{k,l}=\frac{\exp(a_{k,l})}{\sum_{l=1}^{N_{x}}\exp(a_{k,l})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Softmax</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>dis</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\mathrm{Softmax}_{2,{\rm dis}}(\mathbf{A})\right)_{k,l}=\frac{\exp(a_{k,l})}{\sum_{l=1}^{N_{x}}\exp(a_{k,l})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then the updating formula (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In 3.3.1 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) for <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1/M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the discrete setting is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{1/M}=\mathbf{u}^{0}+\mathrm{Softmax}_{2,{\rm dis}}\left(\mathbf{Q}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})(\mathbf{K}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0}))^{\top}\right)\mathbf{V}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Softmax</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>dis</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{1/M}=\mathbf{u}^{0}+\mathrm{Softmax}_{2,{\rm dis}}\left(\mathbf{Q}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})(\mathbf{K}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0}))^{\top}\right)\mathbf{V}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We emphasis again that this is exactly the attention layer in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. This is just a compact mathematical expression for it. All the matrices <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{V}^{Q,0},\mathbf{K}^{Q,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{V}^{Q,0},\mathbf{K}^{Q,0}</annotation></semantics></math> will be learned in the training process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.2 </span>Computing the Discrete Solutions <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2/M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(4+J)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(4+J)/M}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, for any <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}=\{v_{k,l}\}_{k,l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}=\{v_{k,l}\}_{k,l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we discretize <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{v})=\frac{1}{L_{y}}\sum_{l=1}^{N_{y}}v_{k,l},\ \beta_{k}(\mathbf{v})=\frac{1}{L_{y}}\sum_{l}(v_{k,l}-\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{v}))^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>l</mi></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{v})=\frac{1}{L_{y}}\sum_{l=1}^{N_{y}}v_{k,l},\ \beta_{k}(\mathbf{v})=\frac{1}{L_{y}}\sum_{l}(v_{k,l}-\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{v}))^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,...,N_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,...,N_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then we compute the discrete solutions <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2/M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(4+J)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(4+J)/M}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{2/M}_{k,l}=\sigma_{2}^{0}\frac{u^{1/M}_{k,l}-\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{1/M})}{\sqrt{\beta_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{1/M})}}+\sigma_{1}^{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{2/M}_{k,l}=\sigma_{2}^{0}\frac{u^{1/M}_{k,l}-\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{1/M})}{\sqrt{\beta_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{1/M})}}+\sigma_{1}^{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{n+(4+J)/M}_{k,l}=\sigma_{2}^{0}\frac{u^{(3+J)/M}_{k,l}-\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{(3+J)/M})}{\sqrt{\beta_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{(3+J)/M})}}+\sigma_{1}^{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{n+(4+J)/M}_{k,l}=\sigma_{2}^{0}\frac{u^{(3+J)/M}_{k,l}-\alpha_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{(3+J)/M})}{\sqrt{\beta_{k}(\mathbf{u}^{(3+J)/M})}}+\sigma_{1}^{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,...,N_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,...,N_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,...,N_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,...,N_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.3 </span>Computing the Discrete Solutions <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(3+J)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(3+J)/M}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(3+J)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(3+J)/M}</annotation></semantics></math>, its discrete analogue is computed as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{(3+J)/M}=\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{u}^{(2+J)/M}+\mathbf{u}^{2/M})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{(3+J)/M}=\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{u}^{(2+J)/M}+\mathbf{u}^{2/M}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.4 </span>Computing the Discrete Solutions <math id="S3.SS4.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{(2+j)/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{(2+j)/M}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.3.3 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.3.3 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>), we first compute</p>
<table id="S3.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{u}}^{(2+j)/M}=\mathbf{u}^{(1+j)/M}+\mathbf{u}^{(1+j)/M}\mathbf{W}_{j}^{0}+\bm{b}^{0}_{j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{u}}^{(2+j)/M}=\mathbf{u}^{(1+j)/M}+\mathbf{u}^{(1+j)/M}\mathbf{W}_{j}^{0}+\bm{b}^{0}_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and then update</p>
<table id="S3.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{(2+j)/M}=\mathrm{ReLU}(\bar{\mathbf{u}}^{(2+j)/M})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{(2+j)/M}=\mathrm{ReLU}(\bar{\mathbf{u}}^{(2+j)/M}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The structure of the discretized scheme (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.4.4 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(2+𝑗)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), and it exactly recovers the well-known Transformer architecture introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, see the next section for detailed explanations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S3.F1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(a)
<br class="ltx_break"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03989v1/block.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:528/135;" width="528" height="135"></object>
<br class="ltx_break">(b)
<br class="ltx_break"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03989v1/network.svg" id="S3.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:376/104;" width="376" height="104"></object></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) Illustration of the discretized operator-splitting scheme (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). (b) Illustration of the whole evolution.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Mathematical Explanations of Transformers</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Connections to Transformer Encoder in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transformer architecture was first proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> for natural language processing.
After time and spatial discretization, our splitting scheme recovers the well-known Transformer with single-head attention in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. We supply the details of the explanations in the following.
Let us first show that one iteration <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{0}\rightarrow\mathbf{u}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{0}\rightarrow\mathbf{u}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> realizes one Transformer block:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>: With our discretization, <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is an input with <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. Each token is represented as an integer-vector with <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> numbers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaled dot-product attention: Equation (<a href="#S3.E29" title="In 3.4.1 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{1/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) is a single head attention with a skip connection.
The second term in the right-hand side of (<a href="#S3.E29" title="In 3.4.1 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{1/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) is a single-head attention with query matrix <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, key matrix <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and value matrix <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose weight matrices are <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{W}^{K,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{W}^{K,0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{V,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{V,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. It recovers the single-head attention in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. The first term in the right-hand side is a skip connection, adding together the input and output of attention.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Layer normalization: Equation (<a href="#S3.E31" title="In 3.4.2 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{2/𝑀} and 𝑢^{(4+𝐽)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E32" title="In 3.4.2 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{2/𝑀} and 𝑢^{(4+𝐽)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) perform layer normalization. For each token, (<a href="#S3.E31" title="In 3.4.2 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{2/𝑀} and 𝑢^{(4+𝐽)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E32" title="In 3.4.2 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{2/𝑀} and 𝑢^{(4+𝐽)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) normalize the input along the embedding direction to have mean <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Position-wise feedforward network: Equation (<a href="#S3.E34" title="In 3.4.4 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(2+𝑗)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E35" title="In 3.4.4 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(2+𝑗)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) realizes a feedforward network with <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> layers activated by ReLU. In particular, (<a href="#S3.E34" title="In 3.4.4 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(2+𝑗)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) can be rewritten as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{u}}^{(2+j)/M}=\mathbf{u}^{(1+j)/M}(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{W}_{j}^{0})+\bm{b}^{0}_{j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{u}}^{(2+j)/M}=\mathbf{u}^{(1+j)/M}(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{W}_{j}^{0})+\bm{b}^{0}_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐈</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the identity matrix.
It is a linear layer with weight matrix <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{W}_{j}^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{W}_{j}^{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and bias <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}^{0}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}^{0}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Equation (<a href="#S3.E34" title="In 3.4.4 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(2+𝑗)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E35" title="In 3.4.4 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(2+𝑗)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) recovers the feedforward network in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Skip connection: Equation (<a href="#S3.E33" title="In 3.4.3 Computing the Discrete Solutions 𝑢^{(3+𝐽)/𝑀} in () ‣ 3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) is a relaxation step in the splitting scheme. It realizes the skip connection by averaging the input and output of the feedforward network.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The discussion above shows that one time step of the splitting scheme supplied in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> realizes the Transformer of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. In Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, the time domain is discretized into <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> time steps. Thus, the whole operator-splitting scheme is equivalent to compositions of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> Transformer blocks. Since the learnable variables <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{n}=\{\mathbf{W}^{Q,n},\mathbf{W}^{K,n},\mathbf{W}^{V,n},\{\mathbf{W}_{j}^{n},\bm{b}_{j}^{n}\}_{j=1}^{J}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{n}=\{\mathbf{W}^{Q,n},\mathbf{W}^{K,n},\mathbf{W}^{V,n},\{\mathbf{W}_{j}^{n},\bm{b}_{j}^{n}\}_{j=1}^{J}\}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on time, they are different for different time steps. Setting <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}=6</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the architecture in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, solving (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3.1 The overall time discretization ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) for the control variables is equivalent to training the network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Connections to ViT in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the Transformer encoder in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, vision Transformer (ViT) was proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for image classification. In ViT, each image is cropped into patches, each of which is taken as a token. ViT has a similar architecture as the Transformer encoder in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, except that it has an additional input embedding layer with learnable parameters, and a linear layer at the end for output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to use (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) to provide a mathematical explanation of ViT, we need to incorporate the additional embedding layer and last linear layer with our algorithm. It can be easily done by introducing data-driven data pre-processing and post-processing steps. For the additional input embedding, we design a pre-processing step to generate the initial condition <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the discrete setting. Denote <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{(N_{x}-1)\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{(N_{x}-1)\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> as the collection of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{x}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{x}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> patches of an image, where each row of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to one flattened patch of dimension <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an embedding matrix and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector where both are learnable. The vector <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the extra learnable embedding. We generate <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}_{0}=F(\mathbf{R})=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{v}_{0}\\
\mathbf{R}\mathbf{E}\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐑𝐄</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}_{0}=F(\mathbf{R})=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{v}_{0}\\
\mathbf{R}\mathbf{E}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The last linear layer in ViT can be taken as a data post-processing. For any <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
<table id="S4.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G(\mathbf{B};\mathbf{u})=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;\mathbf{0}_{1\times(N_{x}-1)}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{u}\mathbf{B}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐮𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G(\mathbf{B};\mathbf{u})=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;\mathbf{0}_{1\times(N_{x}-1)}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{u}\mathbf{B},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0}_{1\times(N_{x}-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0}_{1\times(N_{x}-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a zero vector of size <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{x}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{x}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\theta},\bar{\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\theta},\bar{\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 The overall time discretization ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. We denote</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\theta}=\{\bar{\theta},\mathbf{v}_{0},\mathbf{E},\mathbf{B}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\theta}=\{\bar{\theta},\mathbf{v}_{0},\mathbf{E},\mathbf{B}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">as the collection of all learnable parameters. With the data pre- and post-processing, we define the whole operation</p>
<table id="S4.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{\rm ViT}_{\widetilde{\theta}}=G\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}\circ F." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ViT</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{\rm ViT}_{\widetilde{\theta}}=G\circ\bar{\mathcal{N}}\circ F.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">One can readily check that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{\rm ViT}_{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ViT</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{\rm ViT}_{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the ViT in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Given a dataset <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\mathbf{u}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\mathbf{u}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, we learn all parameters by solving</p>
<table id="S4.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\widetilde{\theta}}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{\rm ViT}_{\widetilde{\theta}}(\mathbf{u}_{i}),\mathbf{v}_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ViT</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\widetilde{\theta}}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell(\bar{\mathcal{N}}^{\rm ViT}_{\widetilde{\theta}}(\mathbf{u}_{i}),\mathbf{v}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">which is equivalent to training a ViT.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Multi-Head Attention</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Mathematical Explanations of Transformers ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the proposed splitting scheme for problem (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is the Transformer architecture with one-head attention. In this section, we extend (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and splitting scheme (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) so that it realizes multi-head attention.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Control Problem with Continuous Head Dimension</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider a continuous-head setting. After discretizations, we will show that it recovers multi-head attention. In the continuous-head setting, we take head as an additional dimension. To accommodate the new dimension, we update the definition of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q},W^{K},W^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q},W^{K},W^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h,t),W^{K}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h,t),W^{V}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h,t),W^{K}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h,t),W^{V}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h,t)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{h}\times[0,+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{y}\times\Omega_{h}\times[0,+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is the variable for the head dimension with domain <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similar to (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2 Integral Transformations for Attention and Feature Extraction ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2 Integral Transformations for Attention and Feature Extraction ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we define</p>
<table id="S5.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},h,t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{Q}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},h,t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{Q}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},h,t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{K}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},h,t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{K}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},h,t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{V}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},h,t;u)={\Big\langle}W^{V}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We update <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},t;u)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2 Integral Transformations for Attention and Feature Extraction ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) to</p>
<table id="S5.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},h,t;u)=\text{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h,t;u),K(\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},\cdot,h,t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma(\mathbf{x},\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},h,t;u)=\text{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h,t;u),K(\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},\cdot,h,t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The control problem (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is updated to</p>
<table id="S5.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{\int_{\Omega_{h}}{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h,t;u),\ V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}dh}_{\mbox{I-a: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer normalization}}\\
\hskip 28.45274pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}}\mbox{ for }t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}).\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>I-a: attention</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>II: layer normalization</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>III: fully connected network</mtext></msub><mtext> for </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{\int_{\Omega_{h}}{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h,t;u),\ V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h,t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}dh}_{\mbox{I-a: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2})}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer normalization}}\\
\hskip 28.45274pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}}\mbox{ for }t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Compared to (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), problem (<a href="#S5.E38" title="In 5.1 Control Problem with Continuous Head Dimension ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) replace I by I-a, which introduces an additional integral with respect to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Time Discretizations Through Operator-Splitting Method</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The time discretizations and operator-splitting scheme for problem (<a href="#S5.E38" title="In 5.1 Control Problem with Continuous Head Dimension ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) are the same as those for problem (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.4 The continuous Transformer ‣ 2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) except that we update Substep 1 to</p>
<table id="S5.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{1/M}-u^{0}=\int_{\Omega_{h}}{\Big\langle}\gamma^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h;u^{0}),V^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h;u^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}dh," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{1/M}-u^{0}=\int_{\Omega_{h}}{\Big\langle}\gamma^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h;u^{0}),V^{0}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},h;u^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}dh,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">whose solution is given as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{1/M}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{1/M}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle u^{0}+\int_{\Omega_{h}}\int_{\Omega_{x}}\mathrm{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h;u^{0}),K^{0}(\eta,\cdot,h;u^{0};){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right)V^{0}(\eta,\mathbf{y},h;u^{0})d\eta dh." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>Softmax</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{0}+\int_{\Omega_{h}}\int_{\Omega_{x}}\mathrm{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}{\Big\langle}Q^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h;u^{0}),K^{0}(\eta,\cdot,h;u^{0};){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}\right)V^{0}(\eta,\mathbf{y},h;u^{0})d\eta dh.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Discrete Solutions of <math id="S5.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1/M}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E40" title="In 5.2 Time Discretizations Through Operator-Splitting Method ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{h}=[0,L_{h}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{h}=[0,L_{h}]</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition to the spatial discretization discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Spatial Discretizations ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we discretize the head domain into <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> grids. For simplicity, we suppose <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{h}=N_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{h}=N_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For function <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{K,0}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{K,0}(\mathbf{y},\widetilde{\mathbf{y}},h)</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q,0}_{k,l,m}=W^{Q,0}(k,l,m),\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}_{m}=\{W^{Q,0}_{k,l,m}\}_{k,l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times N_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q,0}_{k,l,m}=W^{Q,0}(k,l,m),\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}_{m}=\{W^{Q,0}_{k,l,m}\}_{k,l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times N_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}=\{\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}_{m}\}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times N_{y}\times N_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}=\{\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}_{m}\}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{y}\times N_{y}\times N_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{K,0},\mathbf{W}^{V,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{K,0},\mathbf{W}^{V,0}</annotation></semantics></math> similarly. We denote the discretized integral transformation in the updated attention score as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}_{m},\ \mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{K,0}_{m},\ \mathbf{V}^{n}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{V,0}_{m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{Q,0}_{m},\ \mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{K,0}_{m},\ \mathbf{V}^{n}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\mathbf{u}^{0}\mathbf{W}^{V,0}_{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and denote <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{0}(\mathbf{u})=\{\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})\}_{m},\ \mathbf{K}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\{\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})\}_{m},\ \mathbf{V}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\{\mathbf{V}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})\}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}\times N_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{0}(\mathbf{u})=\{\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})\}_{m},\ \mathbf{K}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\{\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})\}_{m},\ \mathbf{V}^{0}(\mathbf{u}^{0})=\{\mathbf{V}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})\}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{x}\times N_{y}\times N_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The inner product <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Big\langle}Q^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h;u^{0}),K^{0}(\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},\cdot,h;u^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Big\langle}Q^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot,h;u^{0}),K^{0}(\widetilde{\mathbf{x}},\cdot,h;u^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is discretized as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\Big\langle}\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}),\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\bar{\Omega}_{y}}=\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})(\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}))^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\Big\langle}\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}),\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}){\Big\rangle}_{\bar{\Omega}_{y}}=\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})(\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}))^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Then the updating formula (<a href="#S5.E40" title="In 5.2 Time Discretizations Through Operator-Splitting Method ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>) for <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1/M}</annotation></semantics></math> is discretized as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{1/M}=\mathbf{u}^{0}+\sum_{m=1}^{N_{h}}\mathrm{Softmax}_{2,{\rm dis}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{L_{x}}}\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})(\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}))^{\top}\right)\mathbf{V}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>Softmax</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>dis</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{1/M}=\mathbf{u}^{0}+\sum_{m=1}^{N_{h}}\mathrm{Softmax}_{2,{\rm dis}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{L_{x}}}\mathbf{Q}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0})(\mathbf{K}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}))^{\top}\right)\mathbf{V}^{0}_{m}(\mathbf{u}^{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Connections to Transformers</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the discussion in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Mathematical Explanations of Transformers ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, with (<a href="#S5.E41" title="In 5.3 Discrete Solutions of 𝑢^{1/𝑀} in () ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>), the operator-splitting scheme for the control problem (<a href="#S5.E38" title="In 5.1 Control Problem with Continuous Head Dimension ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) realizes <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> encoder time steps in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> with multi-head attention. To show this, we only need to demonstrate the equivalence between (<a href="#S5.E41" title="In 5.3 Discrete Solutions of 𝑢^{1/𝑀} in () ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) and the multi-head attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second term in the right-hand side of (<a href="#S5.E41" title="In 5.3 Discrete Solutions of 𝑢^{1/𝑀} in () ‣ 5 Multi-Head Attention ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) sums over the embedding dimension and head dimension. It is a multi-head attention with query matrix <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, key matrix <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and value matrix <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose weight matrices are <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{W}^{K,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{Q,0},\mathbf{W}^{K,0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{V,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{V,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Convolution Transformers for Video and Image Problems</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Image and video data inherently possess rich local spatial structures, which have been effectively exploited by convolutional neural networks (CNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.
Convolutions serve as a powerful mechanism for capturing spatially localized patterns, making them especially well-suited for data defined on Cartesian grids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, Transformer architectures were originally developed for language processing, where the data—represented by functions such as <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math>—often lack explicit spatial structure, as in the case of sentences or token sequences. For such unstructured data, attention-based mechanisms leveraging general integral transforms offer a flexible and effective modeling tool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, when applying Transformers to structured data such as images, videos, or other signals on regular grids, it has been shown that convolutional operations are significantly more efficient for feature extraction. This is due to their locality, weight sharing, and computational efficiency. Notably, convolutions can be interpreted as a special class of integral transformations with translation-invariant kernels and localized support.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thanks to the integral formulation of the <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> operators in our framework (Sections <a href="#S2" title="2 The Continuous Model ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S3" title="3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), it becomes straightforward to specialize these operators to convolutions. This allows our model to seamlessly incorporate convolutional structures, thereby leveraging domain-specific inductive biases and enhancing computational performance when applied to grid-structured data.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Convolution Transformers</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We discuss the case for grayscale images. The framework can be extended to color images and videos by introducing additional variables to <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider functions of interest <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,\mathbf{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,\mathbf{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Omega_{x}\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Omega_{x}\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the index of tokens (image patches), <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\Omega_{y}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\Omega_{y}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are variables for spatial domain (indices of pixel location), and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the time. For each token and any fixed time <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a two-dimensional image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, we define <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},t),W^{K}(\mathbf{y},t),W^{V}(\mathbf{y},t)\in\Omega_{y}\times[0,+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}(\mathbf{y},t),W^{K}(\mathbf{y},t),W^{V}(\mathbf{y},t)\in\Omega_{y}\times[0,+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> as convolution kernels. Operations <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V</annotation></semantics></math> are defined by covolution as</p>
<table id="S6.EGx46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(x,\mathbf{y},t;u)=W^{Q}(\cdot,t)*u(x,\cdot,t)=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{Q}(\bm{\xi},t)u(x,\mathbf{y}-\bm{\xi},t)d\bm{\xi}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(x,\mathbf{y},t;u)=W^{Q}(\cdot,t)*u(x,\cdot,t)=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{Q}(\bm{\xi},t)u(x,\mathbf{y}-\bm{\xi},t)d\bm{\xi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K(x,\mathbf{y},t;u)=W^{K}(\cdot,t)*u(x,\cdot,t)=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{K}(\bm{\xi},t)u(x,\mathbf{y}-\bm{\xi},t)d\bm{\xi}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K(x,\mathbf{y},t;u)=W^{K}(\cdot,t)*u(x,\cdot,t)=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{K}(\bm{\xi},t)u(x,\mathbf{y}-\bm{\xi},t)d\bm{\xi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V(x,\mathbf{y},t;u)=W^{V}(\cdot,t)*u(x,\cdot,t)=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{V}(\bm{\xi},t)u(xb,\mathbf{y}-\bm{\xi},t)d\bm{\xi}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V(x,\mathbf{y},t;u)=W^{V}(\cdot,t)*u(x,\cdot,t)=\int_{\Omega_{y}}W^{V}(\bm{\xi},t)u(xb,\mathbf{y}-\bm{\xi},t)d\bm{\xi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The score function is computed as</p>
<table id="S6.EGx47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma(x,\widetilde{x},t;u)=\text{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}\int_{\Omega_{y}}Q(x,\bm{\xi},t;u)K(\widetilde{x},\bm{\xi},t;u)d\bm{\xi}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma(x,\widetilde{x},t;u)=\text{Softmax}_{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{|\Omega_{y}|}}\int_{\Omega_{y}}Q(x,\bm{\xi},t;u)K(\widetilde{x},\bm{\xi},t;u)d\bm{\xi}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and the output of the attention layer is then given by: <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Big\langle}\gamma(x,\cdot,t;u),V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Big\langle}\gamma(x,\cdot,t;u),V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With the modification above, scheme (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) gives rise to convolutional Transformer. Solvers discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> can still be used to solve subproblems in the new scheme.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Connections to CvT in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Convolutional vision Transformer (CvT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> incorporates Vit with convolutional neural networks (CNN), which utilizes both image local spatial structures (by CNN) and global contexture (by Transformer). CvT consists of two components, convolutional token embedding and convolutional Transformer block. The convolution Transformer discussed in Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Convolution Transformers ‣ 6 Convolution Transformers for Video and Image Problems ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> realizes a one-head CvT with one stage and without convolutional token embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the convolutional token embedding is an additional convolution layer. Our operator-splitting scheme can be easily revised to accomodate this layer.
Let <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{C}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{C}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math> be a convolution kernel and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{C}(\mathbf{x},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{C}(\mathbf{x},t)</annotation></semantics></math> be a bias term. We consider the following control problem</p>
<table id="S6.EGx48" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),\ V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}}_{\mbox{I: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t))}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer norml.}}+\underbrace{W^{C}(\cdot,t)*u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t)+b^{C}(\mathbf{x},t)}_{\mbox{IV: conv. token embedding}}\\
\hskip 28.45274pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}},\quad t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}),\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>I: attention</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>II: layer norml.</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>IV: conv. token embedding</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>III: fully connected network</mtext></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}=\underbrace{{\Big\langle}\gamma(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t;u),\ V(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t;u){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{x}}}_{\mbox{I: attention}}+\underbrace{\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}(t),\sigma_{2}(t))}(u)}_{\mbox{II: layer norml.}}+\underbrace{W^{C}(\cdot,t)*u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t)+b^{C}(\mathbf{x},t)}_{\mbox{IV: conv. token embedding}}\\
\hskip 28.45274pt+\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left({\Big\langle}W_{j}(\cdot,\mathbf{y},t),\ u(\mathbf{x},\cdot,t){\Big\rangle}_{\Omega_{y}}+b_{j}(\mathbf{x},t)\right)+\partial I_{S_{2}}(u)}_{\mbox{III: fully connected network}},\quad t\in(0,T],\\
u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},0)=f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}),\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the score function <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as discussed in Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Convolution Transformers ‣ 6 Convolution Transformers for Video and Image Problems ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>. For the operator-splitting scheme (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), we add two additional substeps before (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). Given <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=6+J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=6+J</annotation></semantics></math> substeps:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substep 1</span>:
Solve <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{u}^{1/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{u}^{1/M}</annotation></semantics></math> from</p>
<table id="S6.EGx49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{1/M}-u^{0}=W^{C,0}(\cdot)*u^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot)+b^{C,0}(\mathbf{x})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{1/M}-u^{0}=W^{C,0}(\cdot)*u^{0}(\mathbf{x},\cdot)+b^{C,0}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substep 2</span>: Compute <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2/M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2/M}</annotation></semantics></math> from</p>
<table id="S6.EGx50" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{2/M}-u^{1/M}=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}^{0},\sigma_{2}^{0})}(u^{2/M})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{2/M}-u^{1/M}=\partial I_{S_{1}(\sigma_{1}^{0},\sigma_{2}^{0})}(u^{2/M}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The remaining intermediate variables <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{3/M},...,u^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{3/M},...,u^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed via (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), respectively. In spatial discretization, <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the number of tokens, i.e., the number of channels in CvT. Equipped with data pre- and post-processing as discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Connections to ViT in ( ) ‣ 4 Mathematical Explanations of Transformers ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, the discretized new operator splitting scheme recovers a one-head one-stage CvT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For a <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-stage CvT, the number of tokens changes from stage to stage. This property can be realized by incorporating the new operator-splitting scheme with a hybrid splitting scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. Specifically, we need to define <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> convolution kernels and a bias for convolutional token embedding. Based on the multigrid idea, these kernels are discretized at different scales, corresponding to various numbers of tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we have introduced a novel operator-theoretic framework that interprets the Transformer architecture as a discretized operator-splitting scheme for a continuous integro-differential equation. Within this formulation, core components of the Transformer—such as self-attention, layer normalization, and feedforward networks—are rigorously derived as substeps in a structured variational process and operator-splitting techniques. This approach establishes a unified mathematical foundation that applies broadly to Transformer-based models, including the original Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, Vision Transformer (ViT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and Convolutional vision Transformer (CvT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. By bridging deep learning architectures with continuous mathematical modeling, our framework advances theoretical understanding and offers a principled pathway for the design, analysis, and control of future neural architectures. We believe this perspective will stimulate further developments in both the theoretical analysis and practical refinement of attention-based models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Explanation of the variables for Transformers</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The input to the Transformer model is usually a sequence. In the context of language processing, the input is usually sentences. Transformers do not process raw text directly. Instead, they break sentences into tokens. A token can be a word (e.g., "hello"), a subword (e.g., "multi" + "grid" = "multigrid"), or even a single character.
Then, each different token is transformed into unique embedding vectors through an embedding layer.
The way text is tokenized affects how efficiently a model understands language.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, we have an input sentence "Life is like a boat", and we treat each word as a token. The embedding layer would transform each token into a <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector. Then, the input sentence is represented by a matrix <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 512</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext='\underset{\text{Input sentence}}{\underbrace{\text{Life is like a boat}}}\rightarrow\underset{\text{Tokens}}{\underbrace{[\text{"Life","is","like","a","boat"}]}}\rightarrow\underset{\text{Embedded matrix}}{\underbrace{U=\begin{bmatrix}\text{Embedding("Life")}\\
\text{Embedding("is")}\\
\text{Embedding("like")}\\
\text{Embedding("a")}\\
\text{Embedding("boat")}\end{bmatrix}}}.' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>Life is like a boat</mtext><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathsize="0.700em">Input sentence</mtext></munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><munder accentunder="true"><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>"Life","is","like","a","boat"</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathsize="0.700em">Tokens</mtext></munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><munder accentunder="true"><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext>Embedding("Life")</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>Embedding("is")</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>Embedding("like")</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>Embedding("a")</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>Embedding("boat")</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathsize="0.700em">Embedded matrix</mtext></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\text{Input sentence}}{\underbrace{\text{Life is like a boat}}}\rightarrow\underset{\text{Tokens}}{\underbrace{[\text{"Life","is","like","a","boat"}]}}\rightarrow\underset{\text{Embedded matrix}}{\underbrace{U=\begin{bmatrix}\text{Embedding("Life")}\\
\text{Embedding("is")}\\
\text{Embedding("like")}\\
\text{Embedding("a")}\\
\text{Embedding("boat")}\end{bmatrix}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In general, a sentence of <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens with <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding would be transformed into a matrix <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{x}\times N_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{x}\times N_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. This matrix <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as the discretization of a continuous function <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t):\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{y}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\times[0,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},t):\Omega_{x}\times\Omega_{y}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\times[0,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are continuous domains, on a structured grid of size <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{x}\times N_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{x}\times N_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, the variable <math id="A1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the position of tokens, <math id="A1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the entry of token vectors, and <math id="A1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the time variable. The embedded matrix <math id="A1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is fed into the Transformer model and updated by attention layers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h2>

<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Problem (<a href="#S3.E21" title="In 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) can be rewritten as</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx51" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\mathop{\mathrm{argmin}}_{\bar{u}}\frac{1}{2}\|\bar{u}-v\|_{\Omega_{y}}^{2},\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi=\sigma_{1},\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi=\sigma_{2}^{2}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})=\mathop{\mathrm{argmin}}_{\bar{u}}\frac{1}{2}\|\bar{u}-v\|_{\Omega_{y}}^{2},\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi=\sigma_{1},\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi=\sigma_{2}^{2}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Introducing Lagrange multipliers <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the minimizers for the above problem satisfy:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx52" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}(u-v)+\frac{\lambda_{1}}{|\Omega_{y}|}+\frac{2\lambda_{2}}{|\Omega_{y}|}(u-\sigma_{1})=0,\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})d\mathbf{y}=\sigma_{1},\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\mathbf{y}=\sigma_{2}^{2}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}(u-v)+\frac{\lambda_{1}}{|\Omega_{y}|}+\frac{2\lambda_{2}}{|\Omega_{y}|}(u-\sigma_{1})=0,\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})d\mathbf{y}=\sigma_{1},\\
\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\mathbf{y}=\sigma_{2}^{2}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Integrate the first equation over <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx53" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0=\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi-\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi+\lambda_{1}=|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{1}-\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi+\lambda_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0=\int_{\Omega_{y}}u(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi-\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi+\lambda_{1}=|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{1}-\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi+\lambda_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">implying that</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx54" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{1}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi-|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{1}=\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi-|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text">Multiply the first equation in (<a href="#A2.E47" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) by <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u-\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u-\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and then integrate over <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx55" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-v(\mathbf{x},\xi))(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi+2\lambda_{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-v(\mathbf{x},\xi))(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi+2\lambda_{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.6.1" class="ltx_text">from which we deduce</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx56" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-u(\mathbf{x},\xi)\sigma_{1})d\xi-\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi+2\lambda_{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-u(\mathbf{x},\xi)\sigma_{1})d\xi-\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi+2\lambda_{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.7.1" class="ltx_text">Note that</span></p>
<table id="A2.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-u(\mathbf{x},\xi)\sigma_{1})d\xi=\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-2u(\mathbf{x},\xi)\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{1}^{2})d\xi\\
=\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi=|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-u(\mathbf{x},\xi)\sigma_{1})d\xi=\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-2u(\mathbf{x},\xi)\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{1}^{2})d\xi\\
=\int_{\Omega_{y}}(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi=|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.8.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting (<a href="#A2.E50" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>) into (<a href="#A2.E49" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx57" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{2}=-\frac{|\Omega_{y}|}{2}+\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{2\sigma_{2}^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{2}=-\frac{|\Omega_{y}|}{2}+\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{2\sigma_{2}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.9.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting (<a href="#A2.E48" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) and (<a href="#A2.E51" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>) into the first equation of (<a href="#A2.E47" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>), we get</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx58" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u-v)+\frac{\lambda_{1}}{|\Omega_{y}|}+\frac{2\lambda_{2}}{|\Omega_{y}|}(u-\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u-v)+\frac{\lambda_{1}}{|\Omega_{y}|}+\frac{2\lambda_{2}}{|\Omega_{y}|}(u-\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(u-v)+\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi-\sigma_{1}+\left(-1+\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}\right)u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u-v)+\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi-\sigma_{1}+\left(-1+\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}\right)u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\left(-1+\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}\right)\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\left(-1+\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}\right)\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}(u-\sigma_{1})-v+\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}(u-\sigma_{1})-v+\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.10.1" class="ltx_text">We have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx59" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}(u-\sigma_{1})=v-\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}(u-\sigma_{1})=v-\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.11.1" class="ltx_text">Define <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(\mathbf{x}),\beta(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(\mathbf{x}),\beta(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> as in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.3.2 Solution to Subproblem () and () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. We claim that the solution to (<a href="#A2.E53" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>) is given as</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx60" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u=\frac{v-\alpha}{\sqrt{\beta}}\sigma_{2}+\sigma_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>β</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u=\frac{v-\alpha}{\sqrt{\beta}}\sigma_{2}+\sigma_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.12.1" class="ltx_text">To verify it, note that (<a href="#A2.E54" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>) can be rewritten as</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx61" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{u-\sigma_{1}}{\sigma_{2}}=\frac{v-\alpha}{\sqrt{\beta}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>β</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{u-\sigma_{1}}{\sigma_{2}}=\frac{v-\alpha}{\sqrt{\beta}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.13.1" class="ltx_text">Utilizing (<a href="#A2.E55" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>), we deduce</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx62" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}(u-\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})d\xi}{|\Omega_{y}|\sigma_{2}^{2}}(u-\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)\frac{(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})}{\sigma_{2}}d\xi\right)\frac{u-\sigma_{1}}{\sigma_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)\frac{(u(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})}{\sigma_{2}}d\xi\right)\frac{u-\sigma_{1}}{\sigma_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)\frac{(v(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})}{\sqrt{\beta}}d\xi\right)\frac{v-\sigma_{1}}{\sqrt{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msqrt><mi>β</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msqrt><mi>β</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)\frac{(v(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})}{\sqrt{\beta}}d\xi\right)\frac{v-\sigma_{1}}{\sqrt{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|\beta}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}[v^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-2\sigma_{1}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)+\sigma_{1}^{2}]d\xi\right)(v-\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|\beta}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}[v^{2}(\mathbf{x},\xi)-2\sigma_{1}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)+\sigma_{1}^{2}]d\xi\right)(v-\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|\beta}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}(v(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi\right)(v-\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|\beta}\left(\int_{\Omega_{y}}(v(\mathbf{x},\xi)-\sigma_{1})^{2}d\xi\right)(v-\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|\beta}(|\Omega_{y}|\beta)(v-\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|\beta}(|\Omega_{y}|\beta)(v-\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v-\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v-\frac{1}{|\Omega_{y}|}\int_{\Omega_{y}}v(\mathbf{x},\xi)d\xi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.14.1" class="ltx_text">where the second equality follows from (<a href="#A2.E55" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>). The derivation above verifies that (<a href="#A2.E54" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>) is the solution to (<a href="#A2.E53" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>An Introduction to Operator Splitting Schemes</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Operator-splitting methods decompose a complicated time-evolution problem into several substeps so that each substep can be solved efficiently. Consider the evolution equation</p>
<table id="A3.EGx63" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}+\sum_{k=1}^{K}A_{k}(t;u)=0\mbox{ in }\Omega\times(0,T],\\
u(0)=u_{0},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}+\sum_{k=1}^{K}A_{k}(t;u)=0\mbox{ in }\Omega\times(0,T],\\
u(0)=u_{0},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}(t;u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}(t;u)</annotation></semantics></math>’s are operators applied on <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}=n\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}=n\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are three types of splitting strategies to compute <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Sequential Lie scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Sec. 2.2)</cite> decomposes this computation into <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> sequential substeps. Given <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, one computes <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
<br class="ltx_break">For <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,...,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,...,K</annotation></semantics></math>, solve</p>
<table id="A3.EGx64" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}+A_{k}(t;u)=0\mbox{ in }\Omega\times(t^{n},t^{n+1}],\\
u(t^{n})=u^{n+(k-1)/K},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}u_{t}+A_{k}(t;u)=0\mbox{ in }\Omega\times(t^{n},t^{n+1}],\\
u(t^{n})=u^{n+(k-1)/K},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and set <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n+k/K}=u(t^{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n+k/K}=u(t^{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Parallel splitting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> decomposes the computation into <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> parallel substeps:
<br class="ltx_break">For <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,...,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,...,K</annotation></semantics></math>, compute</p>
<table id="A3.EGx65" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}v_{t}+KA_{k}(t;v)=0\mbox{ in }\Omega\times(t^{n},t^{n+1}],\\
v(t^{n})=u^{n},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}v_{t}+KA_{k}(t;v)=0\mbox{ in }\Omega\times(t^{n},t^{n+1}],\\
v(t^{n})=u^{n},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(59)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and set <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}=u(t^{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}=u(t^{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then compute</p>
<table id="A3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n+1}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}v_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n+1}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}v_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Hybrid splitting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> is a combination of sequential and parallel splitting, which decomposes the computation into sequential substeps, each of which contains several parallel substeps.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Substep (<a href="#A3.E58" title="In Appendix C An Introduction to Operator Splitting Schemes ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">58</span></a>) and (<a href="#A3.E59" title="In Appendix C An Introduction to Operator Splitting Schemes ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>) can be solved by one-step explicit or implicit scheme. For example, (<a href="#A3.E58" title="In Appendix C An Introduction to Operator Splitting Schemes ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">58</span></a>) can be solved by</p>
<table id="A3.EGx66" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{u^{n+k/K}-u^{n+(k-1)/K}}{\Delta t}+A_{k}(u^{n+k/K})=0\ \mbox{ or }\ \frac{u^{n+k/K}-u^{n+(k-1)/K}}{\Delta t}+A_{k}(u^{n+(k-1)/K})=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{u^{n+k/K}-u^{n+(k-1)/K}}{\Delta t}+A_{k}(u^{n+k/K})=0\ \mbox{ or }\ \frac{u^{n+k/K}-u^{n+(k-1)/K}}{\Delta t}+A_{k}(u^{n+(k-1)/K})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Our scheme in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> is a sequential splitting scheme, in which (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) uses explicit scheme, and (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) use implicit scheme. Substep (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) is semi-implicit, which is slightly different from others. In fact, (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2 Time Discretizations by Operator Splitting Method ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) is a composition of two substeps, as it is solved by another sequential splitting (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.3.3 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>). In (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.3.3 Solution to Subproblem () ‣ 3.3 Solutions to Each Subproblem ‣ 3 Discretizations of the Continuous Problem ‣ A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers for Large Language Models and GPTs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>), the first substep is explicit and the second one is implicit.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It can be shown that with proper conditions, all three strategies are first-order accurate schemes for the evolution equation.</p>
</div>
</section>
